{
  "outcome": "needs_user_info",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi Leo, thanks for reporting this. I checked your laptop BL-LT-0106 and its main drive is almost full: only about 9 GB of 256 GB is free (3.5%). That is a common cause of slowness. Please try these steps: 1) Empty your Recycle Bin and the Downloads folder. 2) Open Settings > System > Storage > Temporary files, select the items, and remove them. 3) Uninstall any apps you no longer use. 4) If OneDrive is installed, turn on Files On-Demand so synced files stay in the cloud until you open them. 5) Fully restart the laptop (Start > Restart, not just closing the lid). Then reply to this ticket with three things: whether the laptop is faster, roughly how much free space you now have, and whether the slowness began after a Windows update or a new app install. If it is still slow after this, we will escalate it to a senior technician to check whether the laptop needs a repair or replacement.",
  "internal_note": "Checked lookup_user (Leo Martins, Sales, active, not blocked, 0 failed logins, VPN enabled, mailbox 26% used) and check_device_status for BL-LT-0106 (Windows 11 Pro, online, last check-in 2026-10-11 03:25 UTC). Disk: 9.0 GB free of 256 GB, 3.5% free, low disk space warning active. Per KB-017 (under 10% causes problems), advised user cleanup steps and re-check. Per KB-019, still need: last restart date and whether slowness began after an update or app install (KB-028 referenced by KB-019 but not provided in this session; search did not return it). No action tools used; no password or account changes. Escalation trigger from KB-017/KB-019: if free space remains under 10% after cleanup or still slow after restart/updates/cleanup, escalate to Tier 2 (possible drive upgrade, hardware fault, or replacement). Re-run check_device_status after user cleanup to verify.",
  "kb_articles": [
    "KB-017",
    "KB-019"
  ],
  "confidence": "high"
}
